class:: SpecVariation
summary:: Cosine distance between consecutive spectra
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/SpecCentroid2, Classes/SpecFlatness2

description::
Returns 1 - dot(w, previousW) / (norm(w) * norm(previousW)), clipped to 0..1. The first frame and any comparison involving an all-zero spectrum return zero. It retains one spectrum and only advances that history on new FFT frames.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. Peeters (2004), A Large Set of Audio Features for Sound Description; mathematical definitions are implemented independently. Source: link::https://recherche.ircam.fr/anasyn/peeters/ARTICLES/Peeters_2003_cuidadoaudiofeatures.pdf::.

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures cosine distance between consecutive spectra instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
SpecVariation computes the normalized cosine distance between consecutive FFT spectra (ranging from 0 to 1). It measures timbral change independent of absolute energy changes. In this example, sharp spectral transitions (such as sudden percussive hits or timbral shifts) trigger momentary reverse swell delays, creating dynamic rhythmic echoes. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: displays real-time spectral variation.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var variation = SpecVariation.kr(chain, minFreq: 50, maxFreq: 15000, spectrumMode: 0, fftSize: 1024);
        var trig = (variation > 0.35).lag(0.01, 0.1);
        var swell = CombC.ar(source, 0.2, 0.15, 0.8) * trig * 0.4;
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + swell;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, variation.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Spectral Variation", ~miraBus, 0.0, 1.0, "dist");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
